## Generation boundaries

The package provides a secure service skeleton and integration declarations. It does not implement domain entities, state machines, sagas, approval compensation or cross-service idempotency. `ModuleInfoController` supports the first integration check and may be removed when real domain endpoints are ready.

## Integration sequence

1. Review the parent POM, catalog, Gateway, Compose, permission and frontend fragments in `integration/`.
2. From the project root, run `docker compose -p omni-stack-tools -f compose.tools.yaml run --rm cli npm run dev -- service integrate {{serviceId}} --source generated/{{serviceId}}`. After reviewing the plan, append `--apply`. The package path must match the actual generated location.
3. Add forward-only Liquibase changeSets for schema changes. Keep formal seed data in the SQL seed directory.
4. Implement domain rules, permissions and DataScope before exposing business writes.
5. Use the `backend` and `frontend` services in `compose.tools.yaml` to build and lint in local Docker. Then validate Compose and verify 401/403/503 behavior. Run applications inside Docker.

## Documentation and acceptance boundaries

The integration transaction registers all four documents, the Chinese source SHA-256 and the review queue. Generation proves file and structural checks only; it does not establish business acceptance or semantic review. Translations initially remain `present-unverified`. Follow `release_policy` in the target project's `docs/docs-manifest.yaml`: real `AI_REVIEW` authorized by this project's owner satisfies translation review without mandatory human review. Record reviewer, date, source and translation digests, per-document `review_evidence` and its digest. Never label AI review as `HUMAN_REVIEW`.

See the [scaffold guide](guides/scaffold-development.en.md) and [backend patterns](backend-patterns.en.md). Use synthetic data in an isolated Docker project for the first acceptance run. Record requests, expected outcomes and recovery steps; screenshots must exclude tokens and private credentials.

## Handwritten code

The generator does not own domain entities, DTOs, mappers, service implementations or business controllers. After editing a registered generated file, `omni service validate` reports hash drift. Preserve handwritten changes when reviewing a new generation plan.
